        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Buy a treadmill if you already exercise and something specific keeps
            stopping you; do not buy one to start a habit you do not have yet.</strong> Used a couple
            of times a week for a few years, a sensible machine costs a dollar or two a session.
            Used for a few months, it is an expensive clothes rack.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you land in the middle, make the yes reversible: rent, buy used, start with a walking pad
            or buy new from a seller whose return terms you have read. Measure the room first, then
            use our guide to
            <a href="/what-treadmill-should-i-buy/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">what treadmill to buy</a>
            to match the machine to how you will actually use it.
          </p>
